        definition:
          'A bipolar junction transistor is three doped regions forming two junctions back to back, either npn or pnp. Transistor action depends on two deliberate asymmetries: the emitter is heavily doped so it injects carriers efficiently, and the base is made extremely thin and lightly doped so that almost all injected carriers cross it without recombining and are collected by the reverse-biased collector junction. The small fraction that does recombine constitutes the base current. Without the thin base the device would be two isolated diodes, which is exactly what a transistor becomes if the base region is too wide.',
