        definition:
          'A transistor amplifies only in its active region, and a signal applied to an unbiased device would drive it into cut-off for one polarity and produce nothing at all for the other. Biasing establishes a steady DC operating point, the quiescent point, about which the signal can swing in both directions while the device stays active. The Q-point is specified by the quiescent collector current and collector-emitter voltage, and choosing it is a compromise: too close to cut-off and the negative swing clips, too close to saturation and the positive swing clips, and too high a current wastes power and heats the device.',
