        topic: 'Temperature effects on the operating point',
        slides: [29, 30, 31],
        terms: ['VBE temperature coefficient', 'ICO doubling', 'beta with temperature', 'drift', 'three mechanisms'],
        definition:
          'Three separate temperature mechanisms move the operating point, and all three push the collector current in the same direction. The base-emitter voltage falls by about 2 millivolts per degree Celsius, which increases the current for a fixed base drive. The reverse leakage current ICO roughly doubles for every 10 degrees. And beta itself rises with temperature, typically by about half a percent per degree. Because the three reinforce rather than cancel, a circuit that is marginally biased at room temperature can drift substantially in a warm enclosure, which is what bias stabilisation exists to prevent.',
        takeaway: 'Falling VBE, doubling leakage and rising beta all push the current up together — the drift mechanisms never cancel.',
