        terms: ['common base model', 'common collector model', 'impedance comparison', 'no inversion', 'buffer analysis'],
        definition:
          'The same re model serves all three configurations with only the connection points changing. For common base the signal enters at the emitter, so the input impedance is simply re, a few tens of ohms, and the gain is the collector load divided by re with no inversion. For common collector the signal enters at the base and leaves at the emitter, so the input impedance is beta plus one times re plus the emitter resistance, the gain is just below unity, and the output impedance is the source resistance divided by beta plus one. All three results follow from the same two elements arranged differently.',
        takeaway: 'One model, three configurations: what changes is only where the signal enters and leaves.',
        visual: 'three-configurations-one-model',
        visualSpec:
          'Three circuit panels in a row, each built from the same re model elements but with the input and output terminals attached at different points, those terminals highlighted in each case. Beneath each, a results card lists Zi, Zo, Av and Ai. A comparison strip at the bottom plots input impedance on a logarithmic scale for the three, showing common base at tens of ohms, common emitter at kilohms and common collector at hundreds of kilohms, spanning four decades. On reveal: the three panels draw simultaneously with the shared model elements identical, then in each the input and output terminals highlight at their different points, then the three results cards fill in turn, then the impedance comparison strip draws its three markers across the decades.',
        algo: [
          'Draw the same re model in every case',
          'For common base, attach the input at the emitter and the output at the collector',
          'For common emitter, attach the input at the base and the output at the collector',
          'For common collector, attach the input at the base and the output at the emitter',
          'Read off the impedances and gains from the resulting circuit',
        ],
        complexity: { best: 'one model covers all three configurations', avg: 'input impedances span four decades across the three', worst: 'the common base input impedance of tens of ohms loads almost any source', note: 'The four-decade spread in input impedance is the main reason all three configurations survive in practice.' },
        dryRun: {
          input: 'The same transistor at IE = 2 mA with beta = 100, RC = 4 kohm, RE = 1 kohm.',
          steps: [
            're = 13 ohm',
            'Common base: Zi = 13 ohm, Av = 4000/13 = 308, no inversion',
            'Common emitter: Zi = 100 x 13 = 1.3 kohm, Av = -308',
            'Common collector: Zi = 101 x (13 + 1000) = 102 kohm, Av = 0.987',
          ],
          result: 'Input impedances of 13 ohm, 1.3 kohm and 102 kohm from the same device at the same bias. The configuration, not the transistor, sets the impedance.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'math', src: 'Common base:\n  Zi = re,  Zo = RC,  Av = RC/re (no inversion), Ai = alpha\n\nCommon emitter:\n  Zi = beta*re,  Zo = RC,  Av = -RC/re, Ai = beta\n\nCommon collector:\n  Zi = (beta+1)(re + RE),  Zo = re + Rs/(beta+1)\n  Av = RE/(re + RE) ~ 1,   Ai = beta + 1\n\nSame two model elements throughout.' },
        mistake: 'Students learn three separate sets of formulae by rote. All three follow from the same model with different terminal assignments, and deriving them takes less effort than memorising them.',
