          'The decibel expresses a ratio logarithmically: ten times the logarithm of a power ratio, or twenty times the logarithm of a voltage ratio when the impedances are equal. Its value is threefold. Cascaded gains multiply, and logarithms turn that into addition, so a chain of stages is summed rather than multiplied. The enormous dynamic ranges common in electronics compress into manageable numbers. And the human perception of loudness is roughly logarithmic, so the scale matches what is being measured. The convention of twenty for voltage arises because power goes as voltage squared.',
