        definition:
          'At high frequency the gain falls because the capacitances that were open circuits at midband begin to shunt the signal to ground. Two poles dominate: one at the input formed by the source resistance against the sum of the base-emitter capacitance and the Miller capacitance, and one at the output formed by the collector load against the output capacitances and any wiring. The lower of the two poles sets the upper cut-off. Unlike the low-frequency poles, these cannot be moved by choosing larger components, because they arise from the device itself and from the unavoidable stray capacitance of the layout.',
