          'A cascade is simply stages connected in series, each contributing its gain. A cascode is a specific and much cleverer arrangement: a common emitter stage whose collector drives the emitter of a common base stage. The common base input impedance is only re, so the common emitter stage sees a load of about re and therefore has a voltage gain of about one. With unity gain, the Miller multiplication of its base-collector capacitance nearly vanishes, so the input pole moves far higher. The overall voltage gain is still large, because the common base stage supplies it, but it is delivered without the Miller penalty.',
        takeaway: 'Cascode gives the common emitter stage a gain of one so Miller cannot multiply anything, then takes the gain from the common base stage instead.',
        visual: 'cascode-miller-defeat',
        visualSpec:
          'Left: a plain common emitter stage with its Cbc bridging to a high-gain output, the Miller capacitance drawn large at the input and tinted red, with its bandwidth marked low on a small frequency scale. Right: the cascode, with the common emitter transistor below and the common base transistor above, the load on the lower transistor marked as re and its gain annotated "about 1", so the Miller capacitance at its input is drawn small and tinted green, with its bandwidth marked much higher. A shared annotation reads "same total gain, very different bandwidth". On reveal: the plain stage draws with its large red Miller capacitance and low bandwidth marker, then the cascode assembles with the upper transistor appearing, then the re load annotation lands on the lower collector, then the small green Miller capacitance appears and the bandwidth marker slides far right, then the shared annotation lands.',
        algo: [
          'Place a common emitter stage with its collector connected to the emitter of a common base stage',
          'Bias the common base stage so its base sits at a fixed voltage',
          'Note that the common emitter stage now sees a load of approximately re',
          'Compute its voltage gain as approximately one, so the Miller capacitance is roughly 2 Cbc',
          'Take the overall gain from the common base stage driving the collector load',
        ],
        complexity: { best: 'bandwidth improvement of an order of magnitude over a plain common emitter stage', avg: 'two transistors instead of one for the same gain', worst: 'the extra transistor consumes headroom, reducing the available swing', note: 'The cascode is standard in radio-frequency amplifiers and in the output stages of operational amplifiers.' },
        dryRun: {
          input: 'A common emitter stage with Cbc = 4 pF and gain 200, versus the same device in a cascode.',
          steps: [
            'Plain stage: Miller capacitance = 4 x 201 = 804 pF',
            'Cascode lower stage: gain about 1, so Miller capacitance = 4 x 2 = 8 pF',
            'Ratio: 804/8 = 100',
            'The input pole therefore moves roughly a hundred times higher',
          ],
          result: 'The input capacitance falls from 804 pF to 8 pF, moving the bandwidth up by about two orders of magnitude for the same overall gain.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'math', src: 'Cascade: stages in series, gains multiply.\n\nCascode: CE stage driving the emitter of a CB stage.\n  Load on the CE stage = re of the CB stage, tiny\n  => Av(CE) ~ 1\n  => Miller CM = Cbc(1 + 1) = 2*Cbc, not Cbc(1 + 200)\n\nOverall gain comes from the CB stage into RC.\n\nCost: one extra transistor and its headroom.' },
        mistake: 'Students describe a cascode as simply two stages in cascade. The distinguishing feature is that the first stage is deliberately given almost no voltage gain, which is the opposite of what a cascade does and is the entire mechanism.',
