        definition:
          'The feedback in most practical circuits is already present without being labelled. An unbypassed emitter resistor is current-series feedback, which is why it stabilises the gain and raises the input impedance. The collector-feedback bias of Module 2 is voltage-shunt feedback acting at DC and, unless decoupled, at signal frequencies too. A deliberate feedback amplifier usually takes the output of a two-stage amplifier back through a resistive divider to the emitter of the first stage, which gives voltage-series feedback with a beta set by that divider and therefore a closed-loop gain set by resistors alone.',
