        terms: ['phase shift', 'stability', 'phase margin', 'gain margin', 'compensation'],
        definition:
          'Each pole in the amplifier contributes up to 90 degrees of phase lag, so an amplifier with three poles can accumulate 180 degrees. At the frequency where the accumulated phase reaches 180 degrees, the intended negative feedback has become positive; if the loop gain is still one or more at that frequency, the circuit oscillates. Stability is therefore assessed by two margins: the phase margin, the amount by which the phase falls short of 180 degrees where the loop gain is unity, and the gain margin, the amount by which the loop gain is below unity where the phase reaches 180 degrees.',
        takeaway: 'Three poles can supply the 180 degrees that turns negative feedback positive; the margins measure how far you are from that.',
        visual: 'phase-margin-bode',
        visualSpec:
          'A two-panel Bode plot sharing a logarithmic frequency axis. The upper panel plots loop gain in decibels falling through 0 dB at a marked frequency. The lower plots phase falling from 0 towards -270 degrees, with a horizontal reference line at -180 degrees. A vertical line at the unity-gain frequency drops into the phase plot, and the distance from the phase curve there up to -180 degrees is bracketed and labelled "phase margin". A second vertical line where the phase crosses -180 degrees rises into the gain plot, and the distance from the gain curve there down to 0 dB is bracketed and labelled "gain margin". A stability card reads "aim for 45 degrees or more of phase margin". On reveal: the gain curve draws and its 0 dB crossing marks, then the phase curve draws with its -180 reference line, then the unity-gain vertical drops and the phase margin bracket appears, then the -180 vertical rises and the gain margin bracket appears, then the stability card lands.',
