        definition:
          'An oscillator is a feedback amplifier deliberately operated at the point the previous unit warns against. The Barkhausen criterion states that sustained oscillation requires the loop gain magnitude to be exactly one and the total loop phase shift to be zero or a multiple of 360 degrees. The frequency-selective network in the loop determines which frequency satisfies the phase condition, and that is the frequency of oscillation. In practice the loop gain is designed slightly greater than one so oscillation starts from noise, and an amplitude-dependent element then reduces it to exactly one once the output has grown.',
