        definition:
          'A direct-coupled class A stage passes the DC bias current through the load, so half the supply is dropped across the collector resistor and the theoretical maximum efficiency is only 25 percent. A transformer-coupled stage places the load on the secondary, so the primary presents almost no DC resistance and the collector can swing to nearly twice the supply voltage, raising the theoretical maximum to 50 percent. The transformer also transforms the load impedance by the square of the turns ratio, which allows a low-impedance loudspeaker to be matched to the much higher impedance the transistor needs.',
        takeaway: 'The transformer removes the DC drop and matches the impedance, doubling the ceiling from 25 to 50 percent.',
        visual: 'class-a-two-arrangements',
        visualSpec:
          'Two circuits side by side. The left, direct-coupled, shows the load in the collector with the DC current flowing through it and a supply allocation bar beneath showing half the supply lost as DC drop, with the efficiency annotated 25 percent. The right, transformer-coupled, shows the primary in the collector with almost no DC resistance and the load on the secondary, with a load line annotation showing the collector swinging to nearly 2 VCC and the efficiency annotated 50 percent. A turns-ratio formula sits beneath the transformer: R_reflected = n squared times R_load. On reveal: the direct-coupled circuit draws with the DC current path highlighting and its allocation bar filling, then the transformer-coupled circuit draws with its primary highlighted and the extended swing animating on a small load line, then the turns-ratio formula writes beneath.',
