        definition:
          'A junction field effect transistor consists of a channel of doped semiconductor with ohmic contacts at each end, called source and drain, and a gate region of the opposite doping forming a p-n junction along the channel. The gate is always reverse biased, so its junction draws no current but its depletion region extends into the channel and narrows it. Making the gate more negative for an n-channel device widens the depletion region and constricts the channel further, until at the pinch-off voltage the channel is closed entirely. The device is therefore normally on and is turned off by gate voltage, which is the defining depletion-mode behaviour.',
