        definition:
          'The drain characteristic plots drain current against drain-source voltage for a family of gate voltages, and it has two regions. Below pinch-off the channel behaves as a voltage-controlled resistance and the current rises with VDS, giving the ohmic region used in analogue switches. Above pinch-off the current saturates and becomes almost independent of VDS, which is the region used for amplification. The transfer characteristic plots drain current against gate voltage in that saturation region and follows the Shockley square law, a parabola from IDSS at zero gate voltage down to zero at pinch-off.',
