        definition:
          'The common source amplifier is the FET analogue of the common emitter stage: input at the gate, output at the drain, source at signal ground. Its voltage gain is minus gm times the drain load, with the minus sign giving the 180 degree phase inversion, and its input impedance is simply the gate bias resistance, which can be megohms. Leaving the source resistor unbypassed introduces local feedback and reduces the gain to minus gm RD over one plus gm RS, exactly parallel to the BJT case. The gain is generally much lower than a BJT stage because gm is smaller, but the input impedance is orders of magnitude higher.',
