        definition:
          'Operated in the ohmic region below pinch-off, a FET behaves as a resistance whose value is controlled by the gate voltage, which makes it an analogue switch or a voltage-controlled attenuator. Driven hard into that region it becomes a power switch whose on resistance may be milliohms, dissipating very little. The absence of a stored-charge turn-off delay, and the negative temperature coefficient of drain current which makes paralleled devices share current automatically, are the two properties that displaced the bipolar transistor from most power switching applications.',
